content.content:
=== a2_baseline role plan ===
{
  "point_one": {
    "role": "Show that waking without an alarm may be partly a learned, repeatable behavior rather than only a passive function of the circadian clock. The point will connect repeated wake-time practice with individual morningness, while keeping the evidence tied to the specific self-awakening studies.",
    "new_listener_takeaway": "A person who repeatedly wakes near a target time may be developing a practiced timing habit: in one small study, success reached 82% by the seventh day, while a separate survey found that 10.3% of students reported a regular self-awakening habit and tended to keep schedules about 30 minutes earlier. This suggests that consistency and chronotype may shape who experiences this phenomenon most often.",
    "evidence_anchor": "F004 reports the 66-of-643 student survey and its roughly 30-minute earlier schedule; F007 reports an 82% seventh-day success rate among 11 participants who practiced self-awakening for a week; F003 provides the broader review context.",
    "why_it_matters": "It gives listeners a practical interpretation of their own experience: repeated wake times may make natural waking more reliable, but an individual’s morning-or-evening tendency may also influence how easy it feels. The phenomenon is therefore not simply evidence that everyone possesses an equally precise internal alarm.",
    "must_not_overlap_with_full_story": "Do not restate how light and regular sleep schedules entrain the circadian pacemaker, or repeat the pre-awakening ACTH, brain-activity, and heart-rate findings. Do not re-explain the basic claim that the body can prepare for a scheduled waking time.",
    "must_not_overlap_with_other_point": "Do not focus on sleep stages, reduced deep sleep late in the night, or differences in sleep inertia. This point is about practice, repeated timing, and chronotype—not about how easily the brain can transition out of a particular sleep stage."
  },
  "point_two": {
    "role": "Add a sleep-architecture explanation for why a person may be able to wake naturally near the end of a night without feeling as abruptly pulled from sleep. It will distinguish being biologically ready to wake from being able to predict the exact minute.",
    "new_listener_takeaway": "The latter part of a typical night contains less slow-wave sleep and more light and REM sleep, and abrupt waking from deep N3 sleep produces stronger sleep inertia than waking from N2. Thus, a pre-alarm awakening may reflect not only timing signals but also the fact that the final part of the night is generally a more wakeable window.",
    "evidence_anchor": "F002 describes the shift from slow-wave sleep toward lighter and REM sleep across the night; F013 reviews the stronger sleep inertia after awakening from slow-wave sleep; F014 provides experimental N2-versus-N3 evidence from 34 adults.",
    "why_it_matters": "This helps listeners understand why an alarm-free awakening can feel smooth even when it is not evidence of minute-by-minute prediction: the body may be approaching the intended time while sleep itself has become easier to exit. It also explains why waking at the same clock time can feel very different on different nights.",
    "must_not_overlap_with_full_story": "Do not repeat the circadian-entrainment mechanism or the anticipatory ACTH, frontal-brain, and heart-rate changes already used to describe preparation for waking. Do not present lighter sleep as proof that the body has accurately foreseen the alarm time.",
    "must_not_overlap_with_other_point": "Do not discuss training over several days, earlier schedules, morningness, or the prevalence of self-awakening habits. Those belong to Point One; this point should remain focused on sleep stage, wakeability, and sleep inertia."
  }
}

=== b1b_focus role plan ===
{
  "point_one": {
    "role": "Reframe the circadian pacemaker as a synchronizer with a limited range, not an internal stopwatch that can predict any alarm time. This gives the audience a concrete sense of how regular light and sleep schedules can anchor waking without guaranteeing minute-level precision.",
    "new_listener_takeaway": "Under very weak light, a planned activity-rest schedule synchronized the human clock to a 24.0-hour day, but generally not to 23.5- or 24.6-hour cycles. A consistent routine can therefore create a reliable waking window, while the clock still has biological limits on how precisely it can track time.",
    "evidence_anchor": "F001 shows entrainment at 24.0 hours but not usually at 23.5 or 24.6 hours under weak light and scheduled activity-rest cycles; F016 identifies light as the major environmental cue for circadian timing.",
    "why_it_matters": "This helps listeners interpret their own experience more accurately: repeated schedules and morning light may make pre-alarm waking more likely, but an exact wake-up minute should not be treated as proof that the circadian system has read the alarm clock.",
    "must_not_overlap_with_full_story": "Do not repeat the basic explanation that light and regular sleep-wake timing synchronize the circadian system, or restate the overall claim that the body may prepare for a scheduled waking time.",
    "must_not_overlap_with_other_point": "Do not discuss the intentional practice of self-awakening, the 82% day-seven result, or the distinction between passive pre-alarm waking and laboratory self-awakening; those belong to Point Two."
  },
  "point_two": {
    "role": "Add the unexpected idea that self-awakening may involve a learned, expectation-based skill rather than only a fixed biological clock. The point should use repeated practice and individual differences to show how personal routines may shape the phenomenon.",
    "new_listener_takeaway": "In one week-long study of people without an established self-awakening habit, 11 students reached an 82% success rate on the seventh day, while other research links the habit with earlier schedules and morningness. This suggests that repeatedly setting an expected wake time may help recruit a time-aware preparation process, even though it is not proof of exact clock prediction.",
    "evidence_anchor": "F007 reports an 82% success rate on day seven among 11 students practicing self-awakening; F003 documents the heterogeneous 17-study literature, and F004 links self-reported self-awakening with schedules about 30 minutes earlier and a morning-type tendency.",
    "why_it_matters": "Listeners can understand pre-alarm waking as an interaction between biological timing, routine, and expectation—not simply as an automatic ability that some people possess and others lack. That framing also explains why the experience may become more reliable when a person repeatedly plans the same wake-up time.",
    "must_not_overlap_with_full_story": "Do not repeat the main physiological findings about ACTH, prefrontal activity, or heart-rate changes, and do not spend the Point merely saying that self-awakening studies differ from accidental waking before an alarm.",
    "must_not_overlap_with_other_point": "Do not revisit the 24.0-hour entrainment boundary, the role of morning light, or the metaphor of the circadian clock as a non-precision timer; those are Point One's focus."
  }
}

